WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.800
(electronic music)

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:12.640
- (Greg) Hey Wisecrack, Greg here. Today we’re going&nbsp;
to talk about why you don’t really need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:17.920
be afraid of some AI fueled robot with&nbsp;
AK’s for arms coming to snuff you out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.720
No really: Talk about The Terminator series&nbsp;
tends to center around fears of the coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.240
robo-pocalypse. You know, the day when a bunch&nbsp;
of roided-up R2D2s finally come for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.160
- (Kyle) And it absolutely will&nbsp;
not stop, ever! Until you are dead!

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:36.960
- (Greg) But what if the&nbsp;
Terminator is really warning us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.320
not about some far-fetched apocalypse, but&nbsp;
a more subtle&nbsp; danger that’s already here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:48.440
We’ll explain in this Wisecrack edition on the&nbsp;
Terminator series. And, of course, spoilers ahead.

00:01:58.209 --> 00:02:03.600
- (Greg) And now, a quick recap. When an AI system&nbsp;
designed to run America’s defense network becomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.520
self-aware, it decides that the safest course of&nbsp;
action is to nuke the hell out of humanity. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:16.240
honestly, I get it. Anyway, after the mushroom&nbsp;
clouds disperse, a war rages on between man and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.440
machine until the robot army decides to send&nbsp;
a Terminator back in time with one mission:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.320
Kill this perfectly-nice lady, Sarah Conner,&nbsp;
BEFORE she gives birth to the future leader of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:31.120
the human resistance.&nbsp;But humans from the future&nbsp;
send back their own soldier to protect her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:36.080
and the race is on: Who will get to Sarah&nbsp;
Conner first&nbsp;and change the course of history?

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.280
- (Kyle) Come with me if you want to live!

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.640
- (Greg) Subsequent films in the&nbsp;
series have given us good Arnold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.040
bad Arnold and stay-at-home-dad Arnold:

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:48.160
- (Terminator) His name is Mateo. I met his&nbsp;
mother Alysia a few months after I killed John.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.080
- (Greg) But each new installment&nbsp;
revolves around this same basic premise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:58.240
An evil AI tries to kill us all and a robo-soldier&nbsp;
goes back in time to try and save us all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.520
even people who drink hot coffee through straws.&nbsp;
While the sequels have really leaned into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:09.280
sci-fi action thing, the original&nbsp; is essentially&nbsp;
a horror film. Like many classic slashers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.960
it sees a cold-hearted, human-but-not-human&nbsp;
entity stalking relentlessly after what film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.800
scholars call a “Final Girl,” aka the&nbsp;
female character fated to survive all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.320
the villain’s bullshit.&nbsp;Unlike other horror&nbsp;
antagonists, but exactly like Mark Zuckerberg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:32.880
the Terminator puts the cold, ruthless precision&nbsp;
of a computer into a human body. It’s creepy as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.480
all hell. This basic juxtaposition, between&nbsp;
humanity and dead-eyed machine-like logic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.840
is what makes the movie so scary. It also&nbsp;
makes you wonder what Siri’s REALLY capable of.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.480
- (Siri): I could kill you and&nbsp;
they would never find the body.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.720
- (Greg) Personally, that’s way&nbsp;
scarier to me than global nuclear war.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.440
But let’s put killer robots aside. Because&nbsp;
the REAL strength of the series’ commentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.840
is the way it highlights the subtle dangers&nbsp;
already posed by existing technology.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:05.760
The original movie was set, and released,&nbsp;
in 1984, at which point our daily lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.480
had become increasingly automated, and&nbsp;
increasingly reliant on machines.&nbsp; And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:15.680
it’s this dependence on technology that primes&nbsp;
many of the Terminator’s victims for the kill.&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.200
&nbsp;
Take Sarah’s roommate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:22.400
Ginger. While her boyfriend is being attacked&nbsp;
by a burly android invader, she remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:27.840
blissfully unaware thanks to the headphones&nbsp;
blaring those irresistible eighties tunes.

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.285
(glass breaking, music in background)

00:04:30.285 --> 00:04:34.960
- (Greg) Cut to 2020, and we’re so&nbsp;
dependent on technology that I, for one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.640
have basically hot glued my&nbsp;
airpods into my ear holes.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:43.600
And not to sound like a Boomer, but I’d say&nbsp;
that spending most of our lives on screens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.560
often cuts us off from the outside world. And&nbsp;
it’s not just about how you almost got hit by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.920
a car cause you were sexting, either. It’s&nbsp;
also how technology stops working for us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:58.560
Throughout the first movie, the communication&nbsp;
technology that the characters rely on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.840
constantly fails them.&nbsp; Phone calls are&nbsp;
missed, voicemails go unchecked and Ginger’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:09.426
boyfriend embarrassed himself by launching into&nbsp;
a steamy monologue to his girlfriend’s roommate.

00:05:09.426 --> 00:05:12.320
- (Boyfriend) And then... I'll pull&nbsp;
your panties off with my teeth.&nbsp;

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.069
- (Sarah) Who is this?
- (Boyfriend) I uh-

00:05:16.069 --> 00:05:21.200
- (Greg) Happens to the best of us!&nbsp;
I've been there man, believe me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:25.920
And even after the police know that Sarah is&nbsp;
in trouble they still can’t get a hold of her.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.520
- (Officer 1) Call her.
- (Officer 2) I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:28.880
just called her-
- (Officer 1) Call her again!

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:30.560
- (Greg) And when she tries to call them?

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.840
- (Sarah) The police keep transferring me&nbsp;
around. I'm gonna give them another try.

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:38.480
- (Greg) Here, the web of connections that&nbsp;
is our safety net comes unraveled in a time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:45.360
of real crisis. And that was just in 1984. These&nbsp;
days, if the internet went out, the world would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:50.800
probably collapse into chaos.&nbsp;Basically, when&nbsp;
so much of our communication takes place from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:56.720
a distance and is mediated by technology, we’re&nbsp;
left vulnerable in more ways than one.&nbsp;The scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.840
in which Ginger has sex with her boyfriend without&nbsp;
removing her headphones can even be read as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:07.760
a comic indication of how technology has come to&nbsp;
interrupt even our most intimate forms of contact.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:14.080
A study from last year found that 20% of&nbsp;
AirPod users have enjoyed a similar experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.600
hopefully while listening to This American Life.
However, the recording that plays out in response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:23.680
to the police’s unanswered calls&nbsp;
indicates an even deeper problem.&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.160
- (Sarah) Hi there...hahaha fooled&nbsp;
you, you’re talking to a machine.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:34.720
- (Greg) The T-800 tracks Sarah down by&nbsp;
impersonating her mother on the phone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:37.440
showing how easily good technology can trick us.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:38.880
- (Terminator) I love you too sweetheart.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:44.160
- (Greg) Today, Twitterbots and chatbots alike&nbsp;
are both effective because they give us the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.680
sensation of speaking to a real human being.&nbsp;
Of course, whether they’re being used to rile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:55.600
us up or calm us down, they elicit an emotional&nbsp;
response, and that response can be exploited.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.600 --> 00:07:00.960
And this is a fear that becomes more potent&nbsp;
every year. The T-800’s vocal mimicry might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.920
have been sci-fi back in 1984 but it’s&nbsp;
eerily real in the age of Deep Fakes.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:08.320
- (Fake Buscemi) I don't want to have to say&nbsp;
it because who knows when you're going to run&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.873
into these people.
(Crowd laughs)

00:07:10.873 --> 00:07:16.640
- (Greg) Just last year, an AI was able to&nbsp;
impersonate Joe Rogan’s voice well enough to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:21.600
convince listeners they were speaking with&nbsp;
the podcaster himself.&nbsp;At the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:26.160
visual Deep Fakes are becoming an easy way&nbsp;
to spread very convincing misinformation.

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:32.400
- (Fake Obama) President Trump is a total and&nbsp;
complete dip****. Now see I would never say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:40.880
these things, at least not in a public address. But!&nbsp;
Someone else would. Someone like... Jordan Peele.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.560
- (Greg) These days, fear of being lulled into&nbsp;
a false sense of security by computer tricks is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:52.720
very, very real. And Samantha, I don’t care if&nbsp;
you’re actually a 71 year old inmate named Rod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.400
what we had was special and I’ll never regret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:03.680
it.&nbsp;And, of course, unlike in 1984, computers are&nbsp;
now integral to the fabric of our daily lives.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:10.000
This reality was explored in a fun, if not&nbsp;
completely successful sequel from last year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:16.000
Terminator: Dark Fate. Once again, the series&nbsp;
tries to warn us about the very real technological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.080
threats we face today, threats Mackenzie Davis’&nbsp;
robot arm can’t possibly protect us from.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:28.320
Dark Fate puts its hero, Dani, in much the same&nbsp;
position as Sarah and John Connor before her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:34.240
-aware that a single-minded killing machine is on&nbsp;
her trail and desperate to disappear. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:37.920
it’s basically impossible to get off&nbsp;
the grid when the grid is everything.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:43.600
- (Sarah Connor) Now anyone with a phone is a&nbsp;
walking sensor platform. How every intersection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:49.360
every Gas station and 7-11 has cameras? No one&nbsp;
walks through this world without leaving a digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:50.284
trail a mile wide.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.284 --> 00:08:54.400
- (Greg) Throughout the movie, the Rev-9&nbsp;
is able to track its prey by tapping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.680
huge web of internet-connected devices that&nbsp;
now span the globe.&nbsp;In the original film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:07.840
the T-800 happens to be in Sarah’s apartment when&nbsp;
she leaves a voicemail revealing her location.

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:08.480
- (Sarah Connor) Ginger this is&nbsp;
Sarah! Pick up if you're there. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.600
at this place on Pico Boulevard called Tech Noir!

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:17.280
- (Greg) The Rev-9 doesn’t have to rely on such&nbsp;
old-school tricks.&nbsp;Thanks to our smartphones and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:23.120
other devices, we all leave a Godzilla-sized&nbsp;
digital footprint everywhere we go.&nbsp;While it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:28.560
may save us from getting stranded like this,&nbsp;
it also makes it impossible to hide. Whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:34.080
from facial recognition technology or from&nbsp;
Facebook harvesting our very personal data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.080 --> 00:09:37.840
In short, a world in which&nbsp;
computers track our every move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:43.680
isn’t the stuff of futuristic sci-fi, it’s&nbsp;
a world we, and Dani, are already living in.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:44.960
- (Dani) Hey!
- (Sarah) Might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.960 --> 00:09:46.720
as well be wearing a tracking bracelet.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:52.160
- (Greg) But it's not all stolen personal data&nbsp;
and targeted Facebook ads. The Terminator films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:56.400
have always centered around the most&nbsp;
terrifying technological risk of all:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.320
what happens when computers are&nbsp;
the ones with the guns?&nbsp;Ooo!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.040
And before you laugh at the thought of your&nbsp;
Roomba pulling a gun on you,&nbsp;consider this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:13.200
the Korean border is being patrolled by robotic&nbsp;
sentries equipped with actual machine guns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:18.080
Now, to be clear, the manufacturers maintain&nbsp;
that the bots still require human authorization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:22.480
to fire. So we aren’t yet in a world&nbsp;
with fully-automated death machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:27.520
but things sure seem headed that way. As in&nbsp;
Dark Fate, we’ve witnessed an increasing use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:33.360
of lethal drones, particularly by the United&nbsp;
States, which moves us further towards a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.880
mechanized vision of warfare. Recently, the&nbsp;
U.S. agency that researches and develops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:45.505
emerging warfare technology,&nbsp;announced that it&nbsp;
has successfully tested an autonomous drone swarm.

00:10:45.505 --> 00:10:46.160
- (Terminator) It's a far more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:51.200
effective killing machine.
- Oh, great! That's great.

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.360
- (Greg) So, even if we won’t be seeing&nbsp;
anything like a T-800 stomping down the street&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:00.160
any day soon, the military possibilities&nbsp;
of our increasingly advanced computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:06.240
should certainly give us pause. Which brings us&nbsp;
back to the real problem with our continued panic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:11.840
about interconnected networks of murder and&nbsp;
leather-clad killbots: Not only does it freak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.840 --> 00:11:17.760
us the fuck out, but it also distracts from&nbsp;
the much more concrete dangers we face today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.480
The world depicted in the Terminator isn’t&nbsp;
coming any time soon. But there are plenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:28.000
of potential dystopias we could land in along the&nbsp;
way if we’re not careful. From the privacy threat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.040
you carry around in your pocket to that deep fake&nbsp;
of you cheating on your spouse with their dentist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:37.840
the computers which we should be wary of are&nbsp;
already here. But, for all the time it spends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:42.880
teaching us to be terrified of anything with&nbsp;
wires, the Terminator series always ends on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.880 --> 00:11:49.040
a hopeful note. Because technological progress is&nbsp;
not preordained – we get to choose what shape the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:55.840
world of tomorrow will take. Especially if you’re&nbsp;
Elon Musk. Come on, man, don’t f**k this up.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:59.920
At its core, the Terminator series&nbsp;
is about what technology can do to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:05.040
if we’re not careful, and about questioning the&nbsp;
role computers should play in our lives.&nbsp;It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:11.280
great that we can ask Alexa to set a timer&nbsp;
for 4:20 to, um, take our vitamins.&nbsp; It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:15.520
less great when we relinquish all&nbsp;
of our privacy in the process.

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:19.920
So what do you think, Wisecrack? How far is&nbsp;
too far when it comes to computers? Should we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:24.720
keep pressing forward and hope things turn out&nbsp;
more “Next Generation” than “Judgement Day”?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:28.480
Or should we be a little more worried&nbsp;
about the Rise of the Machines?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.640
Let us know in the comments. Big&nbsp;
thanks to our patrons for your support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:37.840
Terminate that subscribe button and,&nbsp;
as always, thanks for watching. Peace!

